WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.020
Take the calcium away from a sheet of cultured epithelial cells.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.040
They round up and let go of one another.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.180
Put it back, and they knit together again.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.380
What was the calcium holding?

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.480
It stiffens the outer arms of adhesion proteins called cadherins, so one cell's arm

6
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.580
can grip the next.

7
00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:28.363
A cadherin crosses the membrane once.

8
00:00:29.103 --> 00:00:32.883
Its outer arm binds the same kind of cadherin on the next cell.

9
00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:39.343
Inside, β-catenin binds the tail directly and recruits α-catenin, which

10
00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:41.423
couples the junction to actin filaments.

11
00:00:42.263 --> 00:00:46.883
A third protein, p120-catenin, binds the tail near the membrane.

12
00:00:47.523 --> 00:00:51.383
So a pull on the outside reaches the cell's own filaments.

13
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:58.167
The second grip is on the extracellular matrix, the woven material cells secrete

14
00:00:58.167 --> 00:00:59.167
around themselves.

15
00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:05.367
The main matrix receptors are integrins, each pairing one α subunit with one

16
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:12.387
β subunit. Mammals have 18 α and 8 β subunits, which pair into 24

17
00:01:12.387 --> 00:01:13.527
different integrins.

18
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:19.207
Many recognise a short sequence, RGD, found in fibronectin.

19
00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:26.067
Inside, talin binds the β tail and links it to actin, and vinculin reinforces the

20
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.000
link.

21
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.780
Not every integrin reaches actin.

22
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.640
Hemidesmosomes pin the basal cells of skin to their basement membrane.

23
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.240
There, integrin α6β4 binds laminin-332 outside the cell.

24
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.700
It connects through plectin to keratin filaments.

25
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.360
Two chains with the same overall shape end on different cables.

26
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:54.013
A cell pulls on its adhesions with myosin II.

27
00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:56.633
Do the links themselves respond?

28
00:01:57.593 --> 00:02:02.333
A purified talin rod is a stack of 13 helical bundles.

29
00:02:03.353 --> 00:02:09.113
Stretched at 12 piconewtons, it showed more vinculin binding events, from one at

30
00:02:09.113 --> 00:02:11.053
most to as many as three.

31
00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:18.683
The reading is that force unfolds bundles and exposes vinculin sites hidden inside them.

32
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:24.103
Autoinhibition is the folded-shut state that hides a protein's binding sites.

33
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:30.763
In mouse fibroblasts, releasing it let talin and vinculin associate, even with

34
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:32.123
myosin II blocked.

35
00:02:33.243 --> 00:02:36.303
Purified fragments show that force can expose sites.

36
00:02:37.143 --> 00:02:40.603
A cell assay shows that activation can do it without force.

37
00:02:41.503 --> 00:02:45.763
Keep the systems apart, because neither says which route dominates in a tissue.
